\title{Tie Profiles and the Ceiling of Spearman's Rho:\\ An Exact Product Bound, the Failure of Headroom Factorisation,\\ and a Ratio Law for Nested Profiles}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-10-25}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
When a predictor (a ``dial'') and a response are recorded with ties, Spearman's rank correlation cannot reach $1$ even under a perfectly monotone relationship. We study the attainable ceiling as a function of the two \emph{tie profiles}, the ordered lists of tied-block sizes. We first prove the two-sided tie correction in variance form: for a profile $L=(l_1,\dots,l_k)$ of $n$ observations, the centred sum of squares of the midranks equals $\big((n^3-n)-\sum_j(l_j^3-l_j)\big)/12$, that is, $(n^3-n)/12$ times the classical tie factor $f(L)=1-\sum_j(l_j^3-l_j)/(n^3-n)\in[0,1]$. From the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality we derive an \emph{exact product bound}: for every pairing of observations, the textbook Spearman coefficient satisfies $\rho_u^2\le f(L)\,f(M)$, with no $O(1/n^2)$ remainder, and with equality for identical aligned profiles. We then show that the natural conjecture that the attainable correlation \emph{factorises} through the two tie factors---which would make dial and response headrooms additive on the log scale---is false: the maximal $\rho_u$ is not a function of $(f(L),f(M))$, and with an untied dial the product bound is never attained by a response with $0<f(M)<1$. In its place we prove a joint-profile law for nested profiles: if one profile coarsens the other, $\max\rho_u=f(\text{coarse})$ and $\max\rho_c=\sqrt{f(\text{coarse})/f(\text{fine})}$ for the tie-corrected coefficient $\rho_c$. Headrooms therefore combine as a ratio, not a product. Applied to a pooled reading of $0.669$ with an untied dial, the results certify $f(M)\ge 0.669$ (textbook reading) or $f(M)\ge 0.447561$ (tie-corrected reading), but they do not allow a reported $+0.045$ advantage to be predicted from the response profile alone. We close with three conjectures supported by exhaustive enumeration for $n\le 7$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Spearman's rank correlation coefficient replaces observations by their ranks and measures how well two rankings agree. It sees only order, so it is invariant under monotone transformations of either variable. In practice, however, data are frequently \emph{tied}: a predictor may take only a few settings, and a response may be recorded on a coarse ordinal scale. The standard remedy assigns each member of a tied block the average of the ranks the block occupies---the \emph{midrank}---and then either keeps the textbook normalisation or renormalises by the actual variances of the midrank vectors.

Either way, ties impose a ceiling: no pairing of observations can produce a rank correlation of $1$ unless the two variables are tied in exactly the same pattern. This paper asks how that ceiling depends on the two tie patterns.

A motivating scenario is the following. A study pools trials in which a dial (an experimenter-controlled ordinal setting) is compared with a response, and reports a pooled Spearman reading of $0.669$. The dial's ties are under the experimenter's control: one can refine the dial until it is untied, driving its tie factor to $1$. A tempting accounting rule---\emph{headroom conservation}---holds that the attainable correlation factorises as the product of a dial factor and a response factor, so that log-headrooms are additive. Under this rule the shortfall of $0.669$ could be charged entirely to the response, and a reported $+0.045$ advantage between two analyses could be predicted from the response tie profile alone.

We show that the first half of this picture is exactly right---there \emph{is} a product ceiling, and it holds with no error term---but that the second half fails: the ceiling is not attained in general, the attainable maximum is not determined by the two tie factors, and in the cleanest tractable case (nested profiles) the two headrooms combine as a \emph{ratio}.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Two-sided tie correction (Theorem~\ref{thm:tie}): an exact closed form for the midrank sum of squares of any tie profile.
\item Exact product bound (Theorem~\ref{thm:product}): $\rho_u^2\le f(L)f(M)$ for every pairing and every $n\ge 2$.
\item Equality and optimal pairing (Propositions~\ref{prop:eq} and~\ref{prop:align}).
\item Failure of factorisation (Theorem~\ref{thm:nonfact}, Corollary~\ref{cor:untied}).
\item Nested-profile law (Theorem~\ref{thm:nested}).
\item Pooled-reading certificate (Corollary~\ref{cor:pooled}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions and notation}

\begin{definition}[Tie profile]
A \emph{tie profile} is a finite sequence $L=(l_1,\dots,l_k)$ of positive integers. Its \emph{size} is $n=\sum_j l_j$. We write $s_j=l_1+\dots+l_{j-1}$ for the number of observations preceding block $j$.
\end{definition}

A profile records the pattern of ties of a variable after sorting. An untied variable has profile $(1,\dots,1)$, written $(1^n)$; a constant variable has the single-block profile $(n)$.

\begin{definition}[Canonical midrank vector]
The midrank vector $R^L=(R^L_1,\dots,R^L_n)$ assigns to each position $i\in\{s_j+1,\dots,s_j+l_j\}$ of block $j$ the value
\[ R^L_i=s_j+\frac{l_j+1}{2}, \]
the average of the integers $s_j+1,\dots,s_j+l_j$. The vector $R^L$ is non-decreasing.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tie mass and tie factor]
The \emph{tie mass} of $L$ is $T(L)=\sum_j(l_j^3-l_j)$. For $n\ge 2$ the \emph{tie factor} is
\[ f(L)=1-\frac{T(L)}{n^3-n}. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rank sum of squares]
With centre $c=(n+1)/2$, $\displaystyle \RSS(L)=\sum_{i=1}^n (R^L_i-c)^2$.
\end{definition}

Let $L,M$ be profiles of the same size $n\ge 2$. Observations are indexed by $i=1,\dots,n$ in the sorted order of the dial, so observation $i$ carries dial midrank $R^L_i$. A \emph{pairing} is a permutation $\sigma$ of $\{1,\dots,n\}$; under $\sigma$, observation $i$ carries response midrank $R^M_{\sigma(i)}$. Every dataset whose two variables have profiles $L$ and $M$ arises from some pairing.

\begin{definition}[Rank covariance and the two coefficients]
\[ \Cov_\sigma(L,M)=\sum_{i=1}^n (R^L_i-c)(R^M_{\sigma(i)}-c),\qquad
\rho_u(\sigma)=\frac{\Cov_\sigma(L,M)}{(n^3-n)/12},\qquad
\rho_c(\sigma)=\frac{\Cov_\sigma(L,M)}{\sqrt{\RSS(L)\,\RSS(M)}}. \]
The \emph{textbook} coefficient $\rho_u$ uses the untied normalisation; the \emph{tie-corrected} coefficient $\rho_c$ is the Pearson correlation of the midrank vectors. We write $\max\rho_u(L,M)=\max_\sigma\rho_u(\sigma)$, and similarly for $\rho_c$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Nested profiles]
A profile $F$ \emph{refines} a profile $C$ of the same size ($C$ \emph{coarsens} $F$) if every block of $C$ is a union of consecutive blocks of $F$. Two profiles are \emph{nested} if one refines the other. The untied profile refines every profile; every profile refines $(n)$.
\end{definition}

\section{The two-sided tie correction}

\begin{lemma}[Moments of midranks]\label{lem:moments}
For every profile $L$ of size $n$,
\[ \sum_{i=1}^n R^L_i=\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\qquad \sum_{i=1}^n (R^L_i)^2=\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}-\frac{T(L)}{12}. \]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on the number of blocks. For $L=(l,L')$ with $n'=n-l$, the midrank vector of $L$ is the constant $(l+1)/2$ on the first $l$ positions followed by $l+R^{L'}$. Hence $\sum_i R^L_i=l\frac{l+1}{2}+ln'+\frac{n'(n'+1)}{2}=\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$. For squares, expand $(l+R^{L'}_x)^2=l^2+2lR^{L'}_x+(R^{L'}_x)^2$, use the induction hypotheses and add $l(\frac{l+1}{2})^2$; a polynomial computation gives the claim. Conceptually, averaging $l$ consecutive integers preserves their sum and lowers their sum of squares by their internal spread $\sum_{t=1}^{l}(t-\frac{l+1}{2})^2=\frac{l^3-l}{12}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided tie correction]\label{thm:tie}
For every profile $L$ of size $n$,
\[ \RSS(L)=\frac{(n^3-n)-T(L)}{12}, \]
and, for $n\ge 2$, $\RSS(L)=\frac{n^3-n}{12}\,f(L)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $(R-c)^2=R^2-(n+1)R+(n+1)^2/4$, sum, and substitute Lemma~\ref{lem:moments}:
\[ \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}-\frac{T(L)}{12}-\frac{n(n+1)^2}{2}+\frac{n(n+1)^2}{4}=\frac{n^3-n}{12}-\frac{T(L)}{12}. \]
Divide by $n^3-n=n(n-1)(n+1)>0$ for the second form.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:range}
For every profile of size $n\ge 2$, $0\le f(L)\le 1$, with $f(L)=1$ iff $L=(1^n)$ and $f(L)=0$ iff $L=(n)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\RSS(L)\ge 0$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:tie} give $f(L)\ge 0$; each $l^3-l=l(l-1)(l+1)\ge0$ gives $f(L)\le 1$. Equality cases: $l^3-l=0$ iff $l=1$, and $\RSS(L)=0$ iff all midranks coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $L=(2,1,1)$: $R^L=(1.5,1.5,3,4)$, $T(L)=6$, $n^3-n=60$, $\RSS(L)=9/2$, $f(L)=9/10$. For $(3,4,3)$: $T=108$ and $f=1-108/990=49/55\approx0.8909$.
\end{example}

Whenever $f(L),f(M)>0$, Theorem~\ref{thm:tie} gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:relation}
\rho_c(\sigma)=\frac{\rho_u(\sigma)}{\sqrt{f(L)\,f(M)}}.
\end{equation}

\section{The product bound}

\begin{theorem}[Exact product bound]\label{thm:product}
Let $L,M$ be profiles of the same size $n\ge 2$. For every pairing $\sigma$,
\[ \Cov_\sigma(L,M)^2\le \RSS(L)\,\RSS(M)\qquad\text{and hence}\qquad \rho_u(\sigma)^2\le f(L)\,f(M). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Cauchy--Schwarz to $u_i=R^L_i-c$ and $v_i=R^M_{\sigma(i)}-c$; re-indexing by the bijection $\sigma$ shows $\sum_i v_i^2=\RSS(M)$. Substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:tie} and divide by $\big(\frac{n^3-n}{12}\big)^2>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The motivating conjecture allowed $\rho^2\le f(L)f(M)+O(1/n^2)$. Theorem~\ref{thm:product} shows the remainder is unnecessary. In terms of~\eqref{eq:relation}, it is the statement $|\rho_c|\le 1$.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Equality for identical aligned profiles]\label{prop:eq}
For every profile $L$ of size $n\ge 2$, $\rho_u(\id)=f(L)$ for the pair $(L,L)$, so $\rho_u(\id)^2=f(L)\cdot f(L)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Cov_{\id}(L,L)=\RSS(L)$; divide by $(n^3-n)/12$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:tie}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Aligned pairing is optimal]\label{prop:align}
For all profiles $L,M$ of the same size, $\max_\sigma\Cov_\sigma(L,M)=\Cov_{\id}(L,M)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:moments}, $\Cov_\sigma(L,M)=\sum_i R^L_iR^M_{\sigma(i)}-nc^2$. Both midrank vectors are non-decreasing, so by the rearrangement inequality the sum is maximised by pairing them in the same order.
\end{proof}

\section{Headroom factorisation fails}

\subsection{The maximum is not a function of the tie factors}

\begin{theorem}[Non-factorisation]\label{thm:nonfact}
There is no function $\Phi:[0,1]^2\to\mathbb{R}$ with $\max\rho_u(L,M)=\Phi(f(L),f(M))$ for all pairs of profiles of equal size. Explicitly, for $n=4$,
\[ f(2,1,1)=f(1,1,2)=\tfrac{9}{10},\quad \max\rho_u\big((2,1,1),(2,1,1)\big)=\tfrac{9}{10},\quad \max\rho_u\big((2,1,1),(1,1,2)\big)=\tfrac45. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both profiles have $T=6$, since the tie mass ignores block order. The first maximum is Proposition~\ref{prop:eq}. For the second, the centred midranks are $u=(-1,-1,\frac12,\frac32)$ and $v=(-\frac32,-\frac12,1,1)$; by Proposition~\ref{prop:align} the maximal covariance is $\frac32+\frac12+\frac12+\frac32=4$, and $(n^3-n)/12=5$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Block averaging and the nested-profile law}

\begin{lemma}[Block averaging]\label{lem:avg}
Let $F$ refine $C$. On every block $B$ of $C$, $\sum_{i\in B}R^F_i=\sum_{i\in B}i=|B|\,R^C_B$, where $R^C_B$ is the common value of $R^C$ on $B$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$B$ is a union of consecutive blocks of $F$, on each of which midranks preserve the sum of positions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nested-profile law]\label{thm:nested}
Let $F$ refine $C$, both of size $n\ge 2$. Then $\max\rho_u(F,C)=\max\rho_u(C,F)=f(C)$, and if $f(C)>0$,
\[ \max\rho_c(F,C)=\sqrt{\frac{f(C)}{f(F)}}. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Proposition~\ref{prop:align} it suffices to treat the aligned pairing. Because centred vectors sum to zero and by Lemma~\ref{lem:avg},
\[ \Cov_{\id}(F,C)=\sum_B R^C_B\sum_{i\in B}(R^F_i-c)=\sum_B|B|\,R^C_B(R^C_B-c)=\RSS(C). \]
Divide by $(n^3-n)/12$, respectively by $\sqrt{\RSS(F)\RSS(C)}$, and use Theorem~\ref{thm:tie}. Cauchy--Schwarz gives $\RSS(C)\le\RSS(F)$, so $f(F)\ge f(C)>0$.
\end{proof}

For nested profiles, $-\log\max\rho_c=\frac12\big[(-\log f(C))-(-\log f(F))\big]$: a \emph{difference} of log-headrooms, not a sum.

\begin{corollary}[Untied dial]\label{cor:untied}
Let $L=(1^n)$ and let $M$ have size $n\ge2$. For every pairing, $\rho_u(\sigma)\le f(M)$ and, if $f(M)>0$, $\rho_c(\sigma)\le\sqrt{f(M)}$, with equality for the aligned pairing. If $0<f(M)<1$ then $\max\rho_u^2=f(M)^2<f(M)=f(L)f(M)$: the product bound is never attained.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(1^n)$ refines $M$ and $f(1^n)=1$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:nested}.
\end{proof}

