\section{The pooled reading}

\begin{corollary}[Certificate from an observed coefficient]\label{cor:pooled}
Suppose the dial is untied and a coefficient $r>0$ is observed. If $r$ is the textbook coefficient then $f(M)\ge r$; if $r$ is the tie-corrected coefficient then $f(M)\ge r^2$. For $r=0.669$: $f(M)\ge0.669$, respectively $f(M)\ge0.447561$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from Corollary~\ref{cor:untied}.
\end{proof}

These are distribution-free finite-sample statements. For orientation, equal-sized response categories on $n=120$ observations have $f(M)=0.750$ (two categories), $0.889$ (three), $0.938$ (four), $0.960$ (five) and $0.990$ (ten); a concentrated response $(1,8,1)$ on $n=10$ has $f(M)\approx0.491$, incompatible with a textbook reading of $0.669$ but compatible with a tie-corrected one.

\paragraph{What does not follow.} The headroom-conservation rule would predict the $+0.045$ advantage from the response tie profile alone. Theorem~\ref{thm:nonfact} shows the attainable maximum depends jointly on both profiles, and Theorem~\ref{thm:nested} shows that even in the nested case the dial's factor enters. Prediction from the response profile alone is valid only when the dial profile refines the response profile. The tie profiles underlying the pooled reading were not available to us, so the value $0.045$ itself is not tested here.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A (aligned ceiling).} Build $R^L,R^M$ block by block; compute $\Cov=\sum_i(R^L_i-c)(R^M_i-c)$; compute $\RSS(L),\RSS(M)$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:tie}; return $\Cov/((n^3-n)/12)$ and $\Cov/\sqrt{\RSS(L)\RSS(M)}$. Correct by Proposition~\ref{prop:align}; cost $O(n)$, exact in rational arithmetic.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (exhaustive census).} Enumerate all $2^{n-1}$ profiles of size $n$ by bitmasks of block boundaries; for every ordered pair apply Algorithm A and test the equality $\max\rho_u^2=f(L)f(M)$, the ratio law, and nestedness. Cost $O(4^n n)$.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (certificate).} Given $r$ and the coefficient type, return the lower bound $r$ or $r^2$ for $f(M)$ and test candidate response profiles.

